The json library in widebench/ is a FROZEN COPY, deliberately.

bench/jsonbench is generated from lib/json by bench/make_jsonbench on every
build and is gitignored, so it tracks the library by construction. This one
does not: its five files were taken at 919d2ef3 (2026-08-14) and committed,
and they now differ from lib/json by 216 lines. bench/encodebench carries the
same snapshot for the same reason.

WHAT THE FREEZE BUYS. With the library held still, a move in this benchmark is
attributable to the COMPILER alone. kanso#1230 is the evidence: it shipped a
library change and a codegen change together, and every unexplained number
attached itself to the larger one until widebench separated them — the raw
byte switch alone, -3.85%, with no library change able to reach it. A
benchmark that tracked lib/json could not have answered that question.

WHAT IT THEREFORE CANNOT SEE. A kernel deleted from lib/json is still measured
here, so these rows are not library coverage and must not be read as such.
bench/jsonbench is what watches the shipped library; this watches the compiler
that builds it.

So a difference between this directory and lib/json is the point rather than a
drift to repair. Re-take the snapshot only deliberately, and say in the commit
which sha it came from and why the old control was retired.
